      "copy": "France’s video game history begins with people encountering a new medium: an arcade machine in a public place, a console connected to a television, or a computer that can be programmed at home. During the 1970s, commercial games establish these new relationships between screen, machine, and player. This is a history of circulation and appropriation, as well as invention.\n\nBy the early 1980s, specialist shops, importers, magazines, and enthusiasts connect French players to a rapidly changing international market. Historical research by Colin Sidre shows why these intermediaries matter: selling and distributing software helps create the conditions for producing it locally. The home computer is both an entertainment device and a workshop.\n\nThe familiar image of a solitary bedroom programmer is therefore only part of the picture. Behind the cassette are places to discover games, people who explain how the machine works, and networks through which a first program can find an audience.",
