      "copy": "Presented on 25 January 1985, the Informatique pour tous plan seeks to bring computing into education and widen access to the machines. Its original presentation connects equipment with teacher training and opportunities for the public to encounter computers. It belongs to a moment when learning to use software, understanding code, and imagining the future of technology become matters of national debate.\n\nFor this exhibition, the plan provides context for the Thomson generation. A computer in a school is not automatically a games studio; an education policy is not a sufficient explanation for an industry. But the initiative helps us understand why these machines occupy such a prominent place in French memories of the 1980s.\n\nAlongside household micros and specialist shops, classrooms become another site of contact with the screen. The history of games overlaps with a broader history of access: who gets to touch a keyboard, learn its language, and imagine making something with it.",
